{-
   The standard library the ownership resolver is built against, and nothing else.

   `compiler/resolve_mir` cannot yet compile `lib/Core`, and a stage with no library at all cannot
   compile a program either: `doc/spec/ownership.md`'s three classes are asked about every type in
   every program, and `[a]` is grammar rather than a call, so what it means has to be reachable
   without being written. This directory is the smallest library that answers both.

   It is a **library, not a mechanism.** The compiler finds it exactly as it finds `lib/` - the same
   walk, the same `Core/Core.yana` test, the same `-lib` override - and every declaration in it binds
   to the compiler the same way `lib/` does, by writing `@builtin(role)` where a role is claimed. So
   nothing here is a stand-in the compiler knows about; it is Core, written small.

   ## What this is for, and how it ends

   Every declaration is a *subset* of the one `lib/Core` writes, with the same name, the same head
   and a signature the real one would accept. Reading the two side by side is the whole of the
   maintenance: `lib/Core/Core.yana` is the source of truth and this file is what fits through the
   stage today.

   It ends by deletion. When `-resolver ownership` compiles the real library, `libraryPath` stops
   defaulting here, this tree goes, and no compiler code changes - which is the property that makes
   it worth having rather than a set of synthesized declarations in C++.
-}

{-
   The operator fixities - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s own block, copied number for number.

   **What an operator does and how it groups are two statements with two sources.** What `+`
   computes is `Num`'s `+`, declared below and selected at the operands' type like any other class
   member; how far `+` reaches is a *declaration*, looked up through the imports like any other
   name, and there is nowhere else for it to come from.

   It used to be a column in a compiler-side table as well, and the two disagreed. `shl` sat one
   rung above `and` there and beside it here, so `a and b shl c` grouped as `a and (b shl c)` under
   this stage and `(a and b) shl c` under the one that reads the real Core - the same source, two
   programs, and nothing to say so. The column is gone and this is what replaced it, which is the
   only arrangement in which the question has one answer.

   The subset is the operators this stage declares. `+=` and its three `Num` siblings are here now,
   with the functions they group; Core also declares `++=`, `??` and `++`, and those group functions
   this file does not declare - a fixity for an operator nothing can call would be a grouping for an
   expression nobody can write. They arrive with the declarations they group.

   Every one is `infixl`, exactly as Core writes it - `doc/spec/expressions.md`, and the reason the
   climb needs no associativity column to be *correct* today rather than merely untested.
-}
infixl 0 +=
infixl 0 -=
infixl 0 *=
infixl 0 /=
infixl 1 ||
infixl 2 &&
infixl 3 ==
infixl 3 !=
infixl 3 >
infixl 3 >=
infixl 3 <
infixl 3 <=
infixl 5 `xor`
infixl 5 `or`
infixl 6 `and`
infixl 6 `shl`
infixl 6 `shr`
infixl 6 `sar`
infixl 6 `rol`
infixl 6 `ror`
infixl 7 +
infixl 7 -
infixl 8 *
infixl 8 /
infixl 8 %

{-
   The names ordinary arithmetic is written in - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s own six, verbatim.

   Nothing about these needed a compiler change: `alias` is an ordinary declaration in this stage,
   `resolveAliasTargets` walks them, and a type name reaches `findAlias` before it reaches
   `findBuiltin`. They were simply not written down, so every fixture said `I32` where the language
   says `Int`.

   **`Int` is a name for a width and not a width of its own**, which is what makes it an alias rather
   than a row in `primitive.def`: a dump prints `I32` for both, because the alias is transparent and
   there is one type behind the two spellings.

   Core also declares `Size`, `USize` and `CodeUnit`, and those are *not* here and could not be -
   they are widths the target answers rather than names for a fixed one, so `primitive.def` declares
   them and this file has nothing to add.
-}
pub alias Int = I32
pub alias Long = I64
pub alias UInt = U32
pub alias ULong = U64
pub alias Float = F32
pub alias Double = F64

{-
   `Bool` - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s own declaration, attribute and all.

   An **ordinary enum**, which is the whole point of it being here: the compiler used to create this
   type itself, on the argument that a comparison has to answer something and this stage might have
   no declarations to answer with. A library it can compile is what removes the argument. What the
   compiler keeps is the *representation* - a two-constructor enum manifests as one byte holding a
   tag, and that is the same answer the builtin gave.

   `@builtin(boolean)` requires the two constructors to be numbered from zero, and this is - which
   makes `False` the zero the comparisons answer with and `True` the one. The words are the
   library's; `compiler/intrinsic` holds neither of them.
-}
@builtin(boolean)
pub data Bool = False | True

{-
   `Ordering` and `Outcome` - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s own two, attribute and all.

   Both are types the compiler *builds values of*: `compare` at a primitive answers an `Ordering`,
   and a skipping lens answers its continuation's result inside an `Outcome`. Neither is a type the
   compiler declares - `@builtin(ordering)` and `@builtin(outcome)` are what let it name these and
   number their constructors, and `Outcome` in particular used to be synthesized per skipping lens
   in C++, with `Proceed` and `Exit` spelled there. `compiler/intrinsic/known.def` says what each
   row requires of the declaration; both are what Core already writes.
-}
@builtin(ordering)
pub data Ordering = LT | EQ | GT

@builtin(outcome)
pub data Outcome(a, e) = Proceed(a) | Exit(e)

{-
   `Maybe` and `Result` - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s own two, verbatim.

   Ordinary generic sum types with nothing said about them anywhere in the compiler, which is why
   they are here at all: the corpus is written in them - a lookup that may find nothing, a call that
   may fail - and every construct they need is one this stage already has. `Nothing` is a nullary
   constructor and therefore a constant; `Just(a)` carries its payload; `match` reaches both by
   constructor and binds what they hold.

   No `@builtin` on either, and that is the point of the pair being here rather than in `known.def`:
   the compiler names neither. What `?` and `?.` dispatch through is `Try` and `Rewrap`, which are
   classes over *any* carrier and are declared below - the compiler names *those*, and these two are
   simply the carriers the corpus is written in.
-}
pub data Maybe(a) = Nothing | Just(a)
pub data Result(e, a) = Err(e) | Ok(a)

{-
   What a carrier says about its two paths - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s `Try` and `Rewrap`, verbatim.

   `?` is the whole of what `Try` is for here: `toOutcome` asks which path a value is on, and
   `fromExit` rebuilds a carrier at the *enclosing function's* return type. That second half is the
   one asymmetry that makes the operator compiler-known rather than a suffix somebody could declare -
   nothing a declaration writes names the return edge it leaves through. Everything else about it is
   ordinary, and a carrier joins by writing an instance.

   `m` decides the other two. An instance head is keyed on it alone and `a` and `e` are read off
   whichever head matched, because nothing at a use site constrains them: `parse(text)?` says what it
   proceeds with only through the instance. That dependency is what turns "which types are these" from
   an inference problem into a lookup.

   Both operands are `->`. Asking which path a value is on is the last thing anyone does with it, so
   consuming it costs nothing and is what keeps the class usable for a carrier whose payload is owned.

   `Rewrap` is the direction `Try` cannot say and is what `?.` needs: a carrier `m` that came in and a
   value `b` the rest of the chain produced, giving the type that is `m`'s wrapper around `b`. Three
   parameters keyed on the first two rather than a constructor applied to a variable, which would be
   the higher-kinded machinery this language fences off; `Maybe(Row)` and `String` give
   `Maybe(String)` by an ordinary head match.
-}
@builtin(carrier)
pub class Try(m -> a, e):
  fn toOutcome(->value: m) -> Outcome(a, e)
  fn fromExit(->reason: e) -> m

instance Try(Maybe(a), a, {}):
  fn toOutcome(->value: Maybe(a)) -> Outcome(a, {}) = match value:
      Just(->inner) -> Proceed(inner)
      Nothing -> Exit({})

  fn fromExit(->reason: {}) -> Maybe(a) = Nothing

instance Try(Result(e, a), a, e):
  fn toOutcome(->value: Result(e, a)) -> Outcome(a, e) = match value:
      Ok(->inner) -> Proceed(inner)
      Err(->reason) -> Exit(reason)

  fn fromExit(->reason: e) -> Result(e, a) = Err(reason)

-- The identity instance, which is what makes `Outcome` itself a usable carrier: something that wants
-- to report *why* it skipped and has no better type to say it in returns this one.
instance Try(Outcome(a, e), a, e):
  fn toOutcome(->value: Outcome(a, e)) -> Outcome(a, e) = value
  fn fromExit(->reason: e) -> Outcome(a, e) = Exit(reason)

@builtin(rewrap)
pub class Rewrap(m, b -> n):
  fn rewrap(->value: b) -> n

instance Rewrap(Maybe(a), b, Maybe(b)):
  fn rewrap(->value: b) -> Maybe(b) = Just(value)

instance Rewrap(Result(e, a), b, Result(e, b)):
  fn rewrap(->value: b) -> Result(e, b) = Ok(value)

instance Rewrap(Outcome(a, e), b, Outcome(b, e)):
  fn rewrap(->value: b) -> Outcome(b, e) = Proceed(value)

{-
   The three ownership classes - `doc/spec/ownership.md`.

   They were synthesized in C++ until this file existed, for the reason that made this file: the
   classifier asks about every type in every program, so a fixture that declares no instance and
   imports nothing still needs them to exist. Written here they are ordinary declarations, checked by
   the ordinary rules, and `instance Drop(T)` in a program is compared against a class rather than
   against a shape test.

   `@builtin` is what binds each to the compiler, and the spelling is `known.def`'s enumerator. The
   real `lib/Core/Core.yana` writes exactly these three attributes on exactly these three classes.
-}
@builtin(reclaim)
pub class Reclaim(a):
    fn reclaim(->value: a) -> {}

@builtin(drop)
pub class Drop(a):
    fn drop(->value: a) -> {}

@builtin(copy)
pub class Copy(a):
    fn copy(from: a) -> a

{-
   What an unadorned literal is - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s two conversion classes, heads and all.

   `1` has no width of its own and `1.5` has no precision of its own, so what each *is* where no
   position asks for anything is a statement the language makes here, in a **parameter default**.
   The compiler used to write the two answers out itself, and the decimal one disagreed with this
   file: `let x = 1.5` was an `F64` under this stage and an `F32` under the one that reads the real
   library. Reading the declaration is the only arrangement in which the question has one answer.

   The members are what a literal *is*: `doc/spec/core.md`'s "a literal is a call to `FromInt`".
   At a primitive the instance is the compiler's - `compiler/intrinsic/provided.def` - and its
   `fromInt` folds a constant argument to a constant of its own type, so `1 :: I32` is `const 1`
   and never a call; at a type a program declares an instance for, `5 :: Money` is `fromInt(5)`;
   and in a generic body, `x + 1` at `a` is a call through the signature's `FromInt(a)`, which
   `Num` already requires of every instance.
-}
@builtin(fromInt)
pub class FromInt(a = Int):
    fn fromInt(value: Long) -> a

@builtin(fromDecimal)
pub class FromDecimal(a = Float):
    fn fromDecimal(value: Double) -> a

{-
   The comparison classes and arithmetic - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s `Eq`, `Ord` and `Num`, members,
   defaults and heads verbatim.

   These are what an operator *is*: `doc/spec/expressions.md` says an operator is "a member of an
   overload set keyed by `(name, arity)`", so `a == b` finds `==` by name exactly as `size(x)` finds
   `size`, and which body runs is instance selection at the operands' type. The compiler adds
   nothing to that for a type a program declares an instance for. What it adds for the primitives
   is the instances themselves - `compiler/intrinsic/provided.def` - because `I32`'s `+` cannot be
   written in terms of anything more basic; and `@builtin(eq)`, `@builtin(ord)`, `@builtin(num)` are
   what let it name the class it is generating an instance of and reach each member by position.

   Three of the compiler's own constructs reach these with no operator written, and each is one
   member: a literal pattern compares with `Eq`'s `==`, a range pattern with `Ord`'s four, and a
   counted `for` tests its bound with `Ord` and steps with `Num` - so `for i in a .. b` counts over
   anything that has both, and not only over the widths.

   The defaults are Core's own and the reason they are kept: an instance that writes `==` has `!=`,
   and one that writes `compare` has all four comparisons. Every primitive instance the compiler
   generates supplies all of these itself, so a `Bool`'s `!=` is the one comparison instruction it
   always was rather than a specialization of `!(lhs == rhs)`.
-}
@builtin(eq)
pub class Eq(a):
  fn ==(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> Bool
  fn !=(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> Bool = !(lhs == rhs)

@builtin(ord)
pub class (Eq(a)) Ord(a):
  fn <(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> Bool = compare(lhs, rhs) == LT
  fn <=(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> Bool = compare(lhs, rhs) != GT
  fn >(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> Bool = compare(lhs, rhs) == GT
  fn >=(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> Bool = compare(lhs, rhs) != LT
  fn compare(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> Ordering
  fn min(->lhs: a, ->rhs: a) -> a = if lhs <= rhs then lhs else rhs
  fn max(->lhs: a, ->rhs: a) -> a = if rhs >= lhs then rhs else lhs

@builtin(num)
pub class (FromInt(a)) Num(a):
  fn +(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn -(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn *(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn /(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn -(value: a) -> a = 0 - value

{-
   The compound assignments - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s four over `Num`, bodies and all.

   Ordinary functions and not syntax: `=` is the one reserved token, so `+=` lexes as an operator
   like `==` does and needs a fixity and a declaration and nothing else. There is no rewrite of
   `x += e` into `x = x + e` anywhere in this stage, and the reason is the mutable borrow - the
   function passes one `&` and walks the property path once, where the rewrite would read and write
   independently.

   Precedence 0 is below every other operator, which is what makes `x += a + b` group as it reads.
   Core's fifth sibling `++=` is over `Append`, which this file does not declare, so it is not here.
-}
pub fn (Num(a)) +=(&target: a, amount: a):
    target = target + amount

pub fn (Num(a)) -=(&target: a, amount: a):
    target = target - amount

pub fn (Num(a)) *=(&target: a, amount: a):
    target = target * amount

pub fn (Num(a)) /=(&target: a, amount: a):
    target = target / amount

{-
   `!`, `&&` and `||` - `lib/Core/Core.yana`'s three plain functions over `Truth`, bodies and all.

   Plain functions rather than class members because what they answer is a `Bool` and what they ask
   is `Truth`, which is the same question `if x` asks - so `if x` and `if !x` cannot disagree about
   a value. The two operands of the pair need not share a type, which a class could not have said.

   `@lazy` on the right operand is what makes short-circuiting a property of these declarations
   rather than of the compiler: the argument is not evaluated at the call site, and reading `rhs`
   is what runs it. What the compiler adds is the `@builtin` role - `compiler/intrinsic/op.def`'s
   `Flow` form - under which an operator written in a body is laid out as a branch with its right
   operand resolved under it, so `a && b` is control flow and no thunk, and `if a && b` is the
   sequence of tests it was already. The bodies are what a call the resolver does not expand
   reaches, and they are the statement of what each operator means.
-}
@builtin(truthNot)
pub fn (Truth(a)) !(value: a) -> Bool = if truthy(value) then False else True

@builtin(truthAnd)
pub fn (Truth(a), Truth(b)) &&(lhs: a, @lazy rhs: b) -> Bool = if truthy(lhs) then truthy(rhs) else False

@builtin(truthOr)
pub fn (Truth(a), Truth(b)) ||(lhs: a, @lazy rhs: b) -> Bool = if truthy(lhs) then True else truthy(rhs)

{-
   The growable array - `[a]`'s other spelling, and the type a window is a window *into*.

   `doc/spec/collections.md`: "`[a]` and `Array(a)` are one type, as `[k:v]` and `Map(k, v)` are".
   So the bracket form has to resolve to a declaration, and this is it; `@builtin(array)` is what
   binds it, and `lib/Core/Array.native.yana` writes the same attribute on the real one.

   **The fields are the library's and the compiler reads none of them.** What the compiler decides
   about this type is the *window* - a shared or unique `[a]` in a binding position, whose two words
   are a manifestation and not a type - and a window points into whatever the owner holds. The real
   Core says `{run: Run(a), length: Count}` natively and `{items: %a, length: @host Count}` on JS,
   which is the same statement made twice with a different first word; this is the JS shape, because
   it is the one that needs no `Run` and no allocator to be written down.

   Nothing can build one yet: there is no allocator in this stage, so an *owned* `[a]` has no
   producer and every occurrence of this type is a window. `newArray`, `push` and the rest arrive
   with the heap.
-}
@builtin(array)
pub data Array(a) {items: %a, length: Size}

{-
   `length(xs)` - what a container's extent is, and the first operation on one.

   Core declares it over `Chunked(c, a)` and this declares the one instance of that shape which needs
   no class: a **window**, which is what every container adapts to. A `[T *n]` argument adapts at the
   call and the extent it adapts with is its own count, so `length` on a fixed array is that number
   and no load - see `compiler/intrinsic/op.def`, where the emitter's side of that is written.

   `@builtin(sequenceLength)` is what binds it; the compiler holds no name for it, so what the
   library calls it is the library's to choose.
-}
@builtin(sequenceLength)
pub fn length(xs: [a]) -> Size
